## §4 State machines

### §4.1 Epistemic status lifecycle (the §4.2 machine)

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> candidate: T1 ingest (IngestNode profile)
    candidate --> canonical: T2 promote
    candidate --> superseded: T3 supersede
    candidate --> refuted: T4 refute
    canonical --> superseded: T3 supersede
    canonical --> refuted: T4 refute
    superseded --> [*]: terminal, RETAINED
    refuted --> [*]: terminal, RETAINED

    candidate --> candidate: T5 recompute confidence
    canonical --> canonical: T5 recompute confidence

    note right of canonical
      T2 GUARD: evidence.minItems >= 1 (C1).
      A reference/frequency count is NEVER
      a promotion input (x5). Frequency may
      only raise review priority.
    end note
    note right of refuted
      Refuted nodes are retained -- a refuted
      decision is high-value knowledge
      (FPL-2). No state removes a node.
    end note
```

| id | Transition | Guard | Schema-enforced? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **T1** | ∅ → `candidate` | document validates against `IngestNode`; `confidence` absent; `namespace` = producing project | ✅ C5 |
| **T2** | `candidate` → `canonical` | `evidence` non-empty **and** the binding is an outcome / benchmark / sealed PAEOS goal | ⚠️ arity only (C1); *kind* of binding is DEF-5 |
| **T3** | `{candidate, canonical}` → `superseded` | a successor node exists carrying a `supersedes` edge; `valid_until` closed | ⚠️ window only (C2); successor existence is DEF-4 |
| **T4** | `{candidate, canonical}` → `refuted` | `falsifier` non-null **and** a matching observation is recorded; `valid_until` closed | ⚠️ C2 + C3; observation binding is DEF-5 |
| **T5** | `s` → `s` | confidence recomputed and propagated along `derived_from` / `supported_by` edges | ❌ DEF-3 (slice S3) |
| **—** | *delete* | **does not exist** | ✅ INV-3 |

`superseded` and `refuted` are **terminal**. A corrected claim enters as a *new* node
bound by a `supersedes` edge; a refuted node is never re-canonicalized in place. This is
the append-only discipline of §4.2 ("Nothing is ever deleted; supersession only") read as
a state machine, and it is what makes `as_of` time-travel (§5.1) sound.

### §4.2 Validity-window sub-machine

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> open: valid_from set, valid_until = null
    open --> closed: valid_until = <ts>  (on T3 or T4)
    closed --> [*]
    note right of open
      An open window is the ONLY legal use of
      valid_until: null. Terminal statuses
      must close it (C2).
    end note
```

Every state has an entry and an exit; `closed` is reachable only through T3/T4, and no
edge returns from `closed` to `open` (reopening would rewrite history).

